UR  - https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6711201
AB  - Oxygen-enriched operation improves the efficiency of large-area proton exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs) but accelerates reversible degradation due to platinum oxidation, sulfur species adsorption, and in-plane water distribution state changes. This study develops an online recovery strategy utilizing short-duration high-current-density pulse (HCDP) with synchronized adjustment of operating parameters to mitigate these degradation modes without interrupting operation. By integrating a physics-based voltage decomposition model, reversible degradation is decomposed into ΔVPtOx,S associated with platinum oxidation and sulfur-functional groups adsorption, and ΔVw caused by in-plane water distribution state changes. HCDP parameter optimization is carried out and recovery mechanisms are revealed: (1) Switching to low-oxygen concentration during the HCDP effectively lowers cathode potential, facilitating the reduction of platinum oxides and desorption of sulfur species; (2) Reducing supply pressure proves superior to increasing flow rate for mitigating flooding (ΔVw), as it enhances gas velocity without incurring additional flow resistance; (3) While elevated temperatures accelerate water evaporation, they pose a risk of membrane dry-out near the inlet, leading to unstable recovery. Under optimized pulse conditions, the proposed strategy achieved 100% recovery of reversible degradation. These findings provide critical theoretical and practical guidance for the durability management of high-efficiency stationary PEMFC systems under oxygen-enriched conditions.
