TI  - Online high-current-density pulse recovery method for oxygen-enriched PEMFCs: Mechanism decoupling and parameter optimization
AU  - Zhang, Xiyuan
AU  - He, Lian
AU  - Xu, Liangfei
AU  - Gan, Quanquan
AU  - Hu, Zunyan
AU  - Li, Jianqiu
AU  - Ouyang, Minggao
PY  - 2026
DO  - 10.2139/ssrn.6384600
UR  - https://doi.org/10.2139/ssrn.6384600
AB  - Proton exchange membrane fuel cells (PEMFCs) aimed at stationary power generation increasingly utilize oxygen-enriched operating conditions with large area to boost efficiency and power. However, this regime accelerates reversible performance degradation due to platinum oxidation, sulfur species adsorption, and in-plane water distribution state changes (localized flooding). This study develops an online recovery strategy utilizing short-duration high-current-density pulse (HCDP) with synchronized adjustment of operating parameters to simultaneously mitigate these degradation modes without interrupting operation. By integrating segmented current density distribution monitoring with a physics-based voltage decomposition model, reversible degradation is decomposed into two components: ΔVPtOx,S, associated with platinum oxidation and sulfur adsorption, and ΔVw, caused by evolution of in-plane water distribution state. A systematic optimization of the HCDP parameters is carried out and recovery mechanisms are revealed: (1) Switching to low-oxygen concentration during the HCDP effectively lowers cathode potential, facilitating the reduction of platinum oxides and desorption of sulfur species; (2) Reducing supply pressure proves superior to increasing flow rate for mitigating flooding (ΔVw), as it enhances gas velocity without incurring additional flow resistance penalties; (3) While elevated temperatures accelerate water evaporation, they pose a risk of membrane dehydration near the inlet, leading to unstable recovery. Under optimized pulse conditions, the proposed strategy achieved 100% recovery of reversible degradation. These findings provide critical theoretical and practical guidance for the durability management of high-efficiency stationary PEMFC systems under oxygen-enriched conditions.
